---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const trigCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'sin(x*pi/180)', label: 'sin θ', color: '#7c3aed', width: 2.2 },
    { expr: 'cos(x*pi/180)', label: 'cos θ', color: '#0f766e' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 720, label: 'θ (degrees)' },
  y: { min: -1.2, max: 1.2 },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};

const trigRatioMatch = {
  "prompt": "Pair each ratio with its definition:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "sin θ",
      "right": "opposite / hypotenuse"
    },
    {
      "left": "cos θ",
      "right": "adjacent / hypotenuse"
    },
    {
      "left": "tan θ",
      "right": "opposite / adjacent"
    }
  ],
  "why": "The real content is that they are functions of angle alone — scale the triangle and the ratios never move."
};

const trigTvEst = {
  "prompt": "A '55-inch' 16:9 TV has width ≈ 47.9 inches. Roughly how wide is it?",
  "unit": "inches",
  "answer": 47.9,
  "factor": 1.15,
  "why": "√((16k)² + (9k)²) = k·√337 ≈ 18.36k = 55, so k ≈ 3.0 — one theorem converts the diagonal on the price tag into whether the screen fits your shelf.",
  "context": "width 16k, height 9k, diagonal 55 inches"
};

const trigPythSpot = {
  "prompt": "One of these claims about Pythagoras is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "a² + b² = c² is exactly the right-angle condition.",
    "For non-right triangles it fails.",
    "The size of the failure is what the cosine rule measures.",
    "a² + b² = c² applies to any triangle."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "a² + b² = c² holds exactly for right angles — for non-right triangles it fails, and the cosine rule supplies the correction term.",
  "why": "The hypotenuse is always the longest side, opposite the right angle — checking this catches swapped-label errors before they happen."
};

const trigLadderEst = {
  "prompt": "A 6 m ladder leans against a wall at 70° to the ground. Roughly how high does it reach?",
  "unit": "m",
  "answer": 5.64,
  "factor": 1.15,
  "why": "6 × sin 70° ≈ 6 × 0.940 = 5.64 m — and the foot sits 6 × cos 70° ≈ 2.05 m from the wall.",
  "context": "to reach a window 5 m up, the required angle is sin⁻¹(5/6) ≈ 56.4°"
};

const trigExactCloze = {
  "text": "The exact values worth knowing cold: 45° (half a square): sin = cos = %%0%%. 60° (half an equilateral triangle): sin = %%1%%/2. 30° (the same triangle from the other corner): sin = %%2%%.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "√2/2",
      "1/√2",
      "0.707"
    ],
    [
      "√3",
      "1.732"
    ],
    [
      "½",
      "1/2",
      "0.5"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "One surd from the square's diagonal, one from the equilateral triangle, and one plain half."
};

const trigCompPredict = {
  "q": "Why is sin θ = cos(90° − θ) always true?",
  "choices": [
    "Complementary angles swap the roles of opposite and adjacent",
    "Sine and cosine are the same function",
    "The unit circle has radius 1"
  ],
  "answer": 0,
  "why": "In the other acute corner of the same triangle, the opposite and adjacent sides exchange — which is the same fact the graph shows as a quarter-period shift."
};

const trigIdBranch = {
  "root": "sin θ = 0.6 for an angle in the first quadrant. What is cos θ?",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "Pick the move:",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "0.8 — from sin²θ + cos²θ = 1",
          "next": "right",
          "feedback": "Right: cos²θ = 1 − 0.36 = 0.64, so cos θ = 0.8 — the circle's equation x² + y² = 1 in trig clothing."
        },
        {
          "label": "0.4 — they must add to 1",
          "next": "add",
          "feedback": "They add to 1 only after squaring: sin²θ + cos²θ = 1, not sin θ + cos θ = 1."
        },
        {
          "label": "Cannot tell without the angle",
          "next": "tell",
          "feedback": "You can: the identity pins cos²θ = 1 − sin²θ, and the quadrant fixes the sign."
        }
      ]
    },
    "right": {
      "text": "Given sin θ, find cos θ — the source of every such manipulation."
    },
    "add": {
      "text": "The squares add to 1; the functions themselves do not."
    },
    "tell": {
      "text": "The identity plus the quadrant is all the information needed."
    }
  },
  "why": "sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 is the unit circle's equation — the most-used fact in the subject."
};

const trigWaveSort = {
  "prompt": "File each effect under the dial that produces it:",
  "buckets": [
    "amplitude (A)",
    "frequency (B)",
    "phase (C)"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "the volume of a sound, the height of a tide",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "a higher note, a faster alternation",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "the lag between two waves",
      "bucket": 2
    }
  ],
  "why": "Reading a formula's A, B and C straight off as 'height, speed, offset' is a skill worth more than memorising any table."
};

const trigRiverOrder = {
  "prompt": "Rebuild the survey that measures a river without crossing — what comes first:",
  "items": [
    "Mark A and B exactly 100 m apart on the bank",
    "Sight the tree C and measure angles A = 30°, B = 45°",
    "Angle C = 105°",
    "AC = 100·sin 45°/sin 105° ≈ 73 m"
  ],
  "why": "No boat required — this is how surveyors, artillery officers and astronomers measured the world for centuries."
};

const trigSineSpot = {
  "prompt": "One of these claims about the sine rule is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "The sine rule: a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C.",
    "It relates each side to the angle opposite it.",
    "The cosine rule is Pythagoras with a correction term for non-right angles.",
    "The sine rule works with any side paired with any angle."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "It only relates a side to the angle opposite it — mismatching the pairs is the most common source of wrong answers in this topic.",
  "why": "a is the side facing A, and so on — the pairing is the rule."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/intermediate/trigonometry">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate mathematics lesson, and it stands on two foundational ones:
    <em>Euclidean Geometry</em>, which supplied similar triangles and the Pythagorean theorem,
    and <em>Linear Functions</em>, whose habit of reading graphs carries over directly. If
    similar triangles — same shape, different scale — feel unfamiliar, revisit that
    foundational lesson first, because the whole subject is one sentence about them: the ratios
    of a right triangle's sides depend only on its angles.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>use sin, cos and tan to find unknown sides and angles in right triangles;</li>
    <li>recall the exact values at 30°, 45° and 60°, and derive them rather than memorise them;</li>
    <li>extend the ratios to all angles with the unit circle, and read their graphs as waves;</li>
    <li>build and read the wave model A·sin(Bθ + C) — amplitude, frequency and phase;</li>
    <li>solve non-right triangles with the sine and cosine rules, and check for the ambiguous case.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Trigonometry starts with a right triangle and the observation that the ratios of its sides
    depend <strong>only on the angles</strong>, never on the triangle's size. Scale a triangle up
    or down and the shape — the set of angles — stays fixed, so "opposite over hypotenuse" stays
    fixed too. Give those three ratios names (sin, cos, tan) and every triangle becomes
    computable from partial information. Then comes the revelation that organises this whole
    chapter: let the angle grow past 90° and keep going, and the ratios trace out
    <strong>waves</strong>. Triangles become circles become waves — the bridge between geometry
    and everything periodic: sound, seasons, tides, alternating current.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="two waves, 90° apart in phase — cos is just sin walked a quarter-turn around the circle" widget="plotter" config={trigCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the panel plots sin θ and cos θ from 0° to 720° — two full
    turns. Trace the purple curve first: it starts at 0, peaks at 1 at 90°, crosses zero at
    180°, and returns to its start at 360°, then repeats — the wave is exactly periodic. The
    teal curve is the same shape sampled 90° earlier: cos peaks where sin crosses zero, which
    is the "quarter-turn" of the caption. Notice too that neither curve ever leaves the band
    between −1 and 1 — a ratio of sides cannot exceed the hypotenuse, so sin and cos are
    trapped there, while tan (not plotted) is not.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the ratios depend only on the angle, and plotted against the angle
    they are waves with period 360°. Before the waves, though, comes the oldest constraint in
    the subject: Pythagoras, and exactly where it applies.
  </p>

  <h2>Pythagoras: the first constraint</h2>
  <p>
    In a right-angled triangle with legs a and b and hypotenuse c, <strong>a² + b² = c²</strong>.
    It is the oldest named theorem in mathematics and the most used: the converse is the
    builder's trick for making a corner square (mark 3, 4 and 5 units along two rays — if the
    distance closes as 5, the angle is exactly right), and the theorem itself is behind every
    distance calculation in coordinate geometry. Real example: a "55-inch" TV has a 16:9 screen.
    If the width is 16k and height 9k, the diagonal is √((16k)² + (9k)²) = k·√337 ≈ 18.36k = 55
    inches, so k ≈ 3.0 inches — width ≈ 47.9", height ≈ 26.9". One theorem converts the
    diagonal on the price tag into whether the screen fits your shelf.
  </p>
  <Widget no="11" caption="call the screen's width" widget="estimate" config={trigTvEst} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a² + b² = c² applies to any triangle. It is exactly the
  <em>right angle</em> condition — for non-right triangles it fails, and the size of the failure
  is precisely what the cosine rule below measures. Also: the hypotenuse is always the
  <em>longest</em> side, opposite the right angle — checking this catches swapped-label errors
  before they happen.</p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="find the overreach" widget="spot-error" config={trigPythSpot} />

  <h2>The three ratios</h2>
  <p>
    For an acute angle θ in a right triangle:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>sin θ = opposite / hypotenuse</strong></li>
    <li><strong>cos θ = adjacent / hypotenuse</strong></li>
    <li><strong>tan θ = opposite / adjacent</strong> (and tan θ = sin θ / cos θ)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    SOH-CAH-TOA packages the definitions; the real content is that they are functions of angle
    alone. Worked example: a 6 m ladder leans against a wall at 70° to the ground. Height
    reached = 6 × sin 70° ≈ 6 × 0.940 = <strong>5.64 m</strong>; distance of the foot from the
    wall = 6 × cos 70° ≈ 6 × 0.342 = <strong>2.05 m</strong>. Reverse problems work the same
    way with an unknown angle: if the ladder must reach a window 5 m up, the required angle is
    sin⁻¹(5/6) ≈ 56.4°. The inverse functions (sin⁻¹, cos⁻¹, tan⁻¹) convert a ratio back into
    an angle — with the fine print that they return one convenient answer, not every possible
    one (see Going deeper).
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="pair each ratio with its sides" widget="match" config={trigRatioMatch} />
  <Widget no="8" caption="call the ladder's reach" widget="estimate" config={trigLadderEst} />

  <h2>Exact values: the angles worth memorising</h2>
  <p>
    Three special angles come from half-squares and half-equilateral triangles, and their sines
    and cosines are exact surds worth knowing cold:
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="fill the blanks — the exact values" widget="cloze" config={trigExactCloze} />
  <ul>
    <li><strong>45°</strong> (half a square): sin = cos = 1/√2 = √2/2, tan = 1.</li>
    <li><strong>60°</strong> (half an equilateral triangle of side 2): sin = √3/2, cos = ½, tan = √3.</li>
    <li><strong>30°</strong> (the same triangle seen from the other acute corner): sin = ½, cos = √3/2, tan = 1/√3.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These are the values behind "nice" answers — the 45° fire-escape, the 60° roof pitch, the
    equilateral truss — and exam questions use them to test understanding without calculator
    noise. They also make the pattern visible: sin 30° = cos 60°. Complementary angles swap the
    roles of opposite and adjacent, so sin θ = cos(90° − θ) always. The graph above shows the
    same fact as a horizontal shift of one quarter-period.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="bet before you read on — the complementary swap" widget="predict" config={trigCompPredict} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the three ratios solve any right triangle, and the special angles
    give exact values — with sin θ and cos(90° − θ) swapping roles. But the definitions were
    built on a triangle with a hypotenuse, and triangles run out at 90°. Something has to
    resurrect them.
  </p>

  <h2>From triangles to waves: the unit circle</h2>
  <p>
    The definitions above die at 90° (no opposite side in a degenerate triangle). The unit
    circle resurrects them: place a point at angle θ on a circle of radius 1, and define
    <strong>cos θ = x-coordinate, sin θ = y-coordinate</strong>. Everything extends smoothly to
    all angles — sin 150° = ½ because the point's height mirrors 30°'s; sin 270° = −1 because
    the point sits at the bottom. The circle's equation x² + y² = 1 becomes, in trig clothing,
    the <strong>Pythagorean identity</strong> sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 — the most-used fact in the
    subject, and the source of every "given sin θ, find cos θ" manipulation.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="the identity at work" widget="branch" config={trigIdBranch} />
  <p>
    Now walk the point around and plot its height against angle: the wave in the plot above.
    Two properties follow immediately. The wave <strong>repeats every 360°</strong> (the point
    returns), and <strong>cos is sin shifted by 90°</strong> (the x-coordinate is the
    y-coordinate's future). Tangent, meanwhile, is the <strong>slope</strong> of the circle at
    that point — which is why tan has period 180° and blows up at 90°, where the circle is
    momentarily vertical.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> all three of sin, cos and tan repeat every 360°. Sin and cos
    do, because a full turn returns the point to its start. Tangent, however, repeats every
    <strong>180°</strong>: it is the slope of the circle, and the circle returns to the same
    slope after only a half-turn — which is why tan 45° and tan 225° are both 1, and why the
    tangent curve has half the period of the other two.
  </p>
  <p>
    The wave picture also gives you a complete family of models for free. Multiplying by a
    constant scales the wave's height (amplitude — volume of a sound, height of a tide);
    multiplying the angle compresses the wave horizontally (frequency — a higher note, a faster
    alternation); adding inside the function shifts it in time (phase — the lag between two
    waves). One formula, A·sin(Bθ + C), is the anatomy of every periodic signal, and reading a
    formula's A, B and C straight off as "height, speed, offset" is a skill worth more than
    memorising any table.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="file each effect under its dial" widget="sort-buckets" config={trigWaveSort} />
  <p>
    <strong>Radians</strong> are the natural angle unit: one radian is the angle subtending an
    arc equal in length to the radius (arc = r × θ, with θ in radians), and a full turn is 2π
    ≈ 6.283 radians. Calculus and physics silently assume radians (the small-angle
    approximation sin θ ≈ θ is false in degrees), and the wave model generalises with them:
    h(t) = A·sin(Bt + C). Worked example: a harbour tide with period 12.4 h (two tidal bulges
    per lunar day), amplitude 1.8 m about a mean of 2.0 m: h(t) = 2.0 + 1.8·sin(2π·t/12.4).
    Set h = 0.5 m (the keel clearance of a yacht) and you can solve for the times of low water —
    a genuinely useful calculation performed by boats worldwide.
  </p>

  <h2>Solving non-right triangles</h2>
  <p>
    Most real triangles lack a right angle, and two rules cover them. The <strong>sine
    rule</strong>, a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, is best when you know an angle and its opposite
    side (ASA, or SSA). Worked example: to measure the width of a river without crossing, mark
    two points A and B exactly 100 m apart on the bank, sight the tree C on the far bank, and
    measure angles A = 30° and B = 45°. Then angle C = 105°, and AC = 100·sin 45°/sin 105° ≈
    100 × 0.7071/0.9659 ≈ <strong>73 m</strong>. No boat required — this is how surveyors,
    artillery officers and astronomers measured the world for centuries.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="rebuild the river-width survey" widget="order" config={trigRiverOrder} />
  <p>
    The <strong>cosine rule</strong>, c² = a² + b² − 2ab·cos C, is Pythagoras with a correction
    term for non-right angles: when C = 90°, cos C = 0 and it collapses to Pythagoras exactly.
    Use it when you know two sides and the included angle (SAS) or all three sides (SSS). Worked
    example: a ship sails 40 km, turns through 65°, and sails 55 km. Distance from port:
    d² = 40² + 55² − 2·40·55·cos 65° = 1600 + 3025 − 4400 × 0.4226 ≈ 2766, so d ≈
    <strong>52.6 km</strong>. The related area formula, Area = ½ab·sin C, gives a triangle's
    area from two sides and the included angle — no height needed.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the sine rule works with any side paired with any angle. It
  only relates a side to the angle <em>opposite</em> it — a, the side facing A, and so on.
  Mismatching the pairs is the most common source of wrong answers in this topic.</p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="find the pairing error" widget="spot-error" config={trigSineSpot} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A 6 m ladder leans at 65° to the ground. How high up the wall does it reach, and how far from the wall is its foot?</li>
    <li>Why is sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 true for every angle, not just acute ones?</li>
    <li>Solve sin θ = 0.5 for 0° to 360°. Why does a calculator only volunteer one answer?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) height = 6 × sin 65° ≈ 5.44 m; foot distance = 6 × cos 65° ≈
    2.54 m — same triangle, opposite-over-hypotenuse and adjacent-over-hypotenuse.
    (2) it is the unit circle's equation x² + y² = 1 in trig clothing: cos θ is the point's
    x-coordinate, sin θ its y-coordinate, and every point on the circle satisfies the equation.
    (3) 30° and 150° — the height ½ occurs twice per turn, mirrored about 90°; the calculator's
    inverse sine returns only the answer between −90° and 90°, so the second solution is your
    job.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Trig ratios depend only on the angle, never on the triangle's size.</li>
    <li>SOH-CAH-TOA: sin = opposite/hypotenuse, cos = adjacent/hypotenuse, tan = opposite/adjacent.</li>
    <li>a² + b² = c² holds exactly for right angles — the cosine rule is Pythagoras plus a correction term for the rest.</li>
    <li>The unit circle extends sin and cos to every angle: cos is the x-coordinate, sin the y-coordinate.</li>
    <li>sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 is the circle's equation; sin and cos repeat every 360°, tan every 180°.</li>
    <li>A·sin(Bθ + C) models any periodic signal: amplitude, frequency, phase.</li>
    <li>The sine rule pairs each side with its opposite angle; the ambiguous case (SSA) can hide two legal triangles.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The wave model you have just built is the shared language of the intermediate tier:
    <em>Circles and Coordinate Geometry</em> deepens the unit-circle picture, and <em>The
    Derivative</em> measures how fast the wave changes — where the small-angle approximation
    stops being a trick and becomes a theorem. In the advanced tier, <em>Differential
    Equations</em> is where sin and cos stop merely describing oscillations and start solving
    them.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Why sin(a+b) is not sin a + sin b.</strong> The addition formulas — sin(a+b) =
    sin a·cos b + cos a·sin b — are the algebraic engine of the subject, provable by dropping a
    perpendicular in a cleverly split triangle, and they generate everything else: the double
    angle formulas (sin 2θ = 2 sin θ cos θ), the wave-product identities, and the "R-form" that
    converts a·sin x + b·cos x into a single shifted wave. Physically, they are the mathematics
    of interference: two waves added is one wave with a new amplitude and phase, which is why
    noise-cancelling works and why the plot's two curves relate by a shift. A useful
    counterexample to memorise: sin(60° + 60°) = sin 120° = √3/2 ≈ 0.866, but sin 60° + sin 60°
    ≈ 1.732. Formulas beat guesses.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The ambiguous case.</strong> Given SSA (two sides and a non-included angle), the
    sine rule can return two valid triangles — the swinging side can land on either of two
    spots. Concretely: a = 8, b = 10, A = 40° gives B ≈ 53.5° <em>or</em> B ≈ 126.5°, both
    geometrically legal. This is why calculators' inverse-sine only returns angles in −90° to
    90°, and why surveyors carry the caution: always check both solutions against the diagram
    before committing. Ambiguity is not error — it is the geometry telling you the data were
    incomplete.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Small angles and why radians rule.</strong> For small θ in radians, sin θ ≈ θ and
    cos θ ≈ 1 − θ²/2 — accurate to within 1% up to about 14°. This single approximation
    underwrites the pendulum's isochronism, the analysis of every small oscillation in physics,
    and the reason your binoculars' "field of view in degrees" converts to "metres at 1,000 m"
    with tan θ ≈ θ. Degrees are a human convention; radians are what the formulas want.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Solving trigonometric equations — and why there are always two answers.</strong> Ask
    when sin θ = 0.5 and a calculator volunteers θ = 30°. But the unit circle shows the height
    is ½ at 30° <em>and</em> at 150° — and at 30° + 360°k forever after. The graph supplies the
    symmetry facts that generate every solution from one: sin is symmetric about 90°, cos about
    0°, tan about 0° with period 180°. Exam problems testing "solve for 0° to 360°" are really
    testing whether you can see the second answer past the calculator's first. The same
    visual habit resolves the inverse-function subtlety from earlier: sin⁻¹(0.5) returns 30°,
    and knowing to add "…or 150°" is the mark of someone who understands the function, not just
    the button.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>A 2,200-year-old measuring project.</strong> Hipparchus (c. 150 BC) built the first
    chord tables to compute eclipses; Indian mathematicians invented the sine as such
    (<em>jya</em>, "bowstring", corrupted through Arabic <em>jayb</em> into Latin "sinus");
    medieval navigators turned spherical trigonometry into ocean-crossing technology — the
    spherical versions of the sine rule are what a GPS constellation still solves, just with
    satellites instead of stars. Every triangle you will ever solve is a small chapter of one
    continuous project: measuring what cannot be walked to.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Algebra and Trigonometry (ch. 7–9); Maor, Trigonometric Delights; NOAA tide prediction methodology (harmonic constituents); standard exact-value derivations. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
